{
    "title": "Explicit Representation of Point Set",
    "pathToSources": "Courses/MathForComputerGraphics/Chapter 1/Block 1/",
    "steps": [
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Hey there, in this part we’re going to explore how to define a set of points explicitly.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "An explicit representation can be as simple as listing the points in the set.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "For example: {[0,0], [1,2], [2,4], [3,6]} is a set of four points.",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": true,
                "properties": {
                    "gridBounds": {
                        "minX": -1,
                        "maxX": 5,
                        "minY": -1,
                        "maxY": 7
                    },
                    "showGridLines": true,
                    "showAxes": true,
                    "showNumbers": true
                },
                "geometryObjects": {
                    "Add": [
                        {
                            "id": "pt0",
                            "objectType": "Point",
                            "pointData": {
                                "position": [
                                    0,
                                    0
                                ],
                                "label": "P0"
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "pt1",
                            "objectType": "Point",
                            "pointData": {
                                "position": [
                                    1,
                                    2
                                ],
                                "label": "P1"
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "pt2",
                            "objectType": "Point",
                            "pointData": {
                                "position": [
                                    2,
                                    4
                                ],
                                "label": "P2"
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "pt3",
                            "objectType": "Point",
                            "pointData": {
                                "position": [
                                    3,
                                    6
                                ],
                                "label": "P3"
                            }
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Another, more interesting, way is to specify a formula or rule that generates each point in a set.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "For example, the explicit representation of a linear function can look like this \n{[x, y] | y = x + 2}.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Notice that the condition expresses y directly in terms of x.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "This explicit equation tells us how to compute the y value by putting in x values.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "It’s like a juicer: you put in an orange (x), press it, and the machine gives you orange juice (y).",
            "contentType": "Image",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "imageData": {
                "imageName": "juicer.png",
                "height": 400
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Let’s take a look at how the explicit form of a line helps us plot the points step by step.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Fill in this table for the line y = x + 2. Start with x = -1.",
            "contentType": "Table",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "table": {
                "header": [
                    "x",
                    "y"
                ],
                "data": [
                    [
                        "-1",
                        ""
                    ],
                    [
                        "2",
                        ""
                    ],
                    [
                        "4",
                        ""
                    ]
                ],
                "highlightedCells": [
                    1
                ]
            },
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "0",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Check again. y = x + 2 when x = -1."
                    },
                    {
